{"name":"Leaping Lizard","hex":"#447a66","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"leaping","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of leap"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaping"},{"term":"lizard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any reptile of the order Squamata that is not a snake or mosasaur—typically characterised by a rounded torso, a short neck with an elevated head, a long tail and four limbs, although some species are legless.","Lizard skin, the skin of these reptiles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lizard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jumping","blocks":[{"text":"Jumping or leaping is a form of locomotion or movement in which an organism or non-living mechanical system propels itself through the air along a ballistic trajectory. Jumping can be distinguished from running, galloping and other gaits where the entire body is temporarily airborne by the relatively long duration of the aerial phase and high angle of initial launch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Rehbock_auf_der_Ostplate_Spiekeroog_-_Nationalpark_nieders%C3%A4chsisches_Wattenmeer.jpg/330px-Rehbock_auf_der_Ostplate_Spiekeroog_-_Nationalpark_nieders%C3%A4chsisches_Wattenmeer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jumping"},{"title":"Lizard","blocks":[{"text":"Lizard is the common name used for all squamate reptiles that are not snakes, encompassing over 7,000 species. They have a cosmopolitan distribution, ranging across all continents except Antarctica, as well as most oceanic island chains. Extant lizard species vary in size from the 13.5 mm (0.53 in) nano-chameleon and the 14–18 mm (0.55–0.71 in) Jaragua dwarf gecko to the 3-meter-long Komodo dragon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg/330px-Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lizard"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/500px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/1280px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/500px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jumping bottlenose dolphin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATursiops_truncatus_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif/250px-SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif/250px-SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A split leap executed by an acro dancer. This is one of several types of leaps found in dance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASplitLeap.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Frog_limbs.jpg/500px-Frog_limbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Frog_limbs.jpg/500px-Frog_limbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bullfrog skeleton, showing elongate limb bones 